### What is Linked List Cycle II?

Linked List Cycle II goes beyond detecting a cycle. It finds the exact node where the cycle begins, which is the node that has two incoming references: one from the tail of the non-cyclic portion and one from inside the loop. Knowing this node allows you to break the cycle by setting the appropriate `next` pointer to `null`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransactionNode {
    public String txnId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public TransactionNode next;

    public TransactionNode(String txnId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.txnId = txnId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

### How does phase one work?

Phase one is standard Floyd's detection. Start slow and fast at the head. Advance slow by one and fast by two. If they meet, a cycle exists and the meeting point is inside the loop. If fast reaches null, there is no cycle.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;

public class CycleStartFinder {
    public static TransactionNode findCycleStart(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode slow = head;
        TransactionNode fast = head;

        // Phase 1: detect cycle
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            if (slow == fast) {
                // Phase 2: find entry point
                TransactionNode entry = head;
                while (entry != slow) {
                    entry = entry.next;
                    slow = slow.next;
                }
                return entry;
            }
        }
        return null; // no cycle
    }
}
```

### Why does phase two find the cycle start?

Let `a` be the distance from head to the cycle start, and let `b` be the distance from the cycle start to the meeting point. The slow pointer has traveled `a + b` steps. The fast pointer has traveled `2(a + b)` steps. Because the fast pointer also completed some full loops of length `L` inside the cycle:

`2(a + b) = a + b + n * L` for some integer n, which simplifies to `a + b = n * L`, and therefore `a = n * L - b`.

This means the distance from the head to the cycle start (`a`) equals the distance from the meeting point to the cycle start when measured forward around the loop (`n * L - b`). So two pointers starting from the head and the meeting point, both advancing one step at a time, will meet at the cycle start.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CycleStartDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Build: A -> B -> C -> D -> E -> back to C
        TransactionNode a = new TransactionNode("TXN-A", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        TransactionNode b = new TransactionNode("TXN-B", new BigDecimal("2000"));
        TransactionNode c = new TransactionNode("TXN-C", new BigDecimal("3000"));
        TransactionNode d = new TransactionNode("TXN-D", new BigDecimal("4000"));
        TransactionNode e = new TransactionNode("TXN-E", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        a.next = b; b.next = c; c.next = d; d.next = e; e.next = c; // cycle at C

        TransactionNode start = CycleStartFinder.findCycleStart(a);
        System.out.println("Cycle starts at: " + start.txnId);
    }
}
```

```text
Cycle starts at: TXN-C
```

### How is this different from the hash-set approach?

The hash-set approach stores every visited node and returns the first node that is visited twice. It also finds the cycle start in O(n) time but uses O(n) space. Floyd's two-phase method achieves the same result in O(1) space.

| Feature | Floyd's Two-Phase | Hash-Set Approach |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | O(1) | O(n) |
| Finds start directly | After two phases | Immediately on repeat visit |
| Code complexity | Requires math understanding | Straightforward |

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public static TransactionNode findCycleStartHashSet(TransactionNode head) {
    Set<TransactionNode> visited = new HashSet<>();
    TransactionNode current = head;
    while (current != null) {
        if (!visited.add(current)) {
            return current; // first revisited node is cycle start
        }
        current = current.next;
    }
    return null;
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

The main edge cases are: no cycle (return null), single node pointing to itself (cycle start is that node), and cycle starting at the head itself.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CycleStartEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // No cycle
        TransactionNode n1 = new TransactionNode("TXN-1", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        n1.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-2", new BigDecimal("2000"));
        System.out.println("No cycle: " + CycleStartFinder.findCycleStart(n1));

        // Self-loop
        TransactionNode self = new TransactionNode("TXN-SELF", new BigDecimal("500"));
        self.next = self;
        System.out.println("Self-loop: " + CycleStartFinder.findCycleStart(self).txnId);

        // Cycle at head
        TransactionNode h = new TransactionNode("TXN-H", new BigDecimal("100"));
        TransactionNode t = new TransactionNode("TXN-T", new BigDecimal("200"));
        h.next = t; t.next = h;
        System.out.println("Cycle at head: " + CycleStartFinder.findCycleStart(h).txnId);
    }
}
```

```text
No cycle: null
Self-loop: TXN-SELF
Cycle at head: TXN-H
```

### How do you break the cycle once you find the start?

Once you know the cycle start node, you can walk from that node around the loop until you find the node whose `next` points back to the start, and set that `next` to `null`.

```java
public static void breakCycle(TransactionNode head) {
    TransactionNode cycleStart = CycleStartFinder.findCycleStart(head);
    if (cycleStart == null) return;

    TransactionNode runner = cycleStart;
    while (runner.next != cycleStart) {
        runner = runner.next;
    }
    runner.next = null; // break the cycle
    System.out.println("Cycle broken at link from " + runner.txnId + " to " + cycleStart.txnId);
}
```

### What about applications beyond linked lists?

The same two-phase technique applies to any sequence defined by repeatedly applying a function. The classic "find the duplicate number" problem uses array values as links: if `nums[i]` points to the next index, a duplicate creates a cycle, and Floyd's algorithm finds it in O(n) time and O(1) space.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FindDuplicate {
    public static int findDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        int slow = nums[0];
        int fast = nums[0];

        // Phase 1: detect cycle
        do {
            slow = nums[slow];
            fast = nums[nums[fast]];
        } while (slow != fast);

        // Phase 2: find entry
        int entry = nums[0];
        while (entry != slow) {
            entry = nums[entry];
            slow = nums[slow];
        }
        return entry;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] accountIds = {1, 3, 4, 2, 2}; // 2 is duplicate
        System.out.println("Duplicate account ID: " + findDuplicate(accountIds));
    }
}
```

```text
Duplicate account ID: 2
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The elegance of phase two is that the distance equality `a = nL - b` falls directly from the two-to-one speed ratio. A senior engineer should be able to derive this from scratch rather than memorize it. The proof is short: slow travels `a + b`, fast travels `2(a + b)`, and the difference `a + b` must be a multiple of the loop length `L`.

In production at the National Bank of India, this algorithm is used in the chain repair tool. After detecting that an imported transaction chain has a loop, the tool needs the exact re-entry node so it can set the offending `next` pointer to `null` and restore a proper acyclic list.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;

public class ChainRepairTool {
    public static void repairChain(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode cycleStart = CycleStartFinder.findCycleStart(head);
        if (cycleStart == null) {
            System.out.println("Chain is clean, no cycle found.");
            return;
        }
        TransactionNode runner = cycleStart;
        while (runner.next != cycleStart) {
            runner = runner.next;
        }
        System.out.println("Repairing: breaking link from " + runner.txnId
            + " back to " + cycleStart.txnId);
        runner.next = null;
    }
}
```
